{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791447107,"nonce":"NqobG21LRrnRnPJk2iBexqSyZ0E=","title":"Unannotated parameters stored into nested record fields remain Dynamic","message":"Narrow scope from 4f791be88d3ec9ca0e2a0b96f3c3e5524b74f435e1aecc2c14199e0c6b0ba653: Issue5477 has four wrong assertions from D.set(x), where field.value1 = x stores a box address into the Int slot. Array field element stores already infer an Int parameter; nested anonymous fields do not.\n\nParent 7992d7bc7625b1ae5e215993ac8d18f6baf10c6a reproduces this in /tmp/AssignmentParameterProbe.hx: native prints 4,5,6; Rayzor prints 4,\u003cpointer bits\u003e,6 under both default tiered and pinned Cranelift. MIR AssignmentRecord.put takes *void and casts it to i64 before rayzor_anon_set_field_by_index (/tmp/rayzor-assignment-parameters-mir-v3.log). The abstract constructor probe already passes, so Issue2639 is outside this scope.\n\nNative NestedStoreParameters passes (/tmp/rayzor-parameter-stores-native-v1.log); parent fails its first nested Int assertion (/tmp/rayzor-parameter-stores-before-v1.log). The fixture covers Int/Float/Bool/String nested record fields, deep record fields, nested Array\u003cFloat\u003e elements, generic receiver chains, and local/parameter names shadowing the record field. The implementation extends parameter store inference using declaration types through aliases, record fields and array elements; a local or parameter receiver prevents guessing a class field type.\n\nFull corpus and regression validation pending.","files":null}]}